text in the argument. - Apply knowledge of the named enzyme's typical reaction mechanism (e.g. CYP450 hydroxylation/oxidation, UGT glucuronidation, SULT sulfation, MAO deamination, NAT acetylation, etc.). - If multiple enzymes are listed (semicolon-separated), use their combined or most likely reaction on the substrate. Reasoning trace (Claude Sonnet 5.5): a. Functional groups relevant to CYP1A2: The parent contains an N-methyl pyrazole (N–CH3 on the ring nitrogen). CYP1A2 is well known for N-demethylation of aromatic amines and N-alkyl heterocycles via alpha-carbon hydroxylation followed by hemi-aminal collapse. b. Enzyme mechanism: CYP1A2 abstracts a hydrogen from the N-methyl carbon (N–CH3 → N–CH2•), then oxygen rebound gives N–CH2OH; the hemi-aminal spontaneously collapses to the free N–H and formaldehyde. c. Site of biotransformation: The 1-methyl (N1-CH3) on the pyrazole is the primary target. The C3-methyl is on a carbon, not nitrogen, and is far less reactive. d. Chemical transformation: N-Demethylation: –N(CH3)– → –NH–, loss of CH2O. e. Structural change to SMILES: Original N-methyl: Cc1nn(C)c(Cl)c1… ← the nn(C) nitrogen loses its CH3 Product: Cc1[nH]nc(Cl)c1… ← becomes [nH] Tool call: submit_metabolite({"smiles": "Cc1[nH]nc(Cl)c1C(O)=Nc1cccc2c1C(C)OC2(C)C"}) Getting started with code The recipes below are drawn from the nemo-rl and nemo-gym training modules. Each snippet is self-contained and can be adapted to a new domain by replacing the corresponding configs. NemoGym environment registration The following snippets register a NemoGym RLVR environment from an experimental CSV. Registering the environment takes four pieces. 1. CSV to Gym JSONL Each CSV row becomes one JSONL row. The extra keys, ground_truth_smiles here, are passed to the server’s seed_session and verified. import csv import json SYSTEM = open("system_prompt.txt").read() TOOLS = [{ "type": "function", "name": "submit_metabolite", "strict": True, "description": ( "Submit your predicted metabolite SMILES. Returns validity and Tanimoto" " similarity (0-1)." ), "parameters": { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": False, "required": ["smiles"], "properties": { "smiles": { "type": "string", "description": "Predicted metabolite SMILES (no extra text).", } }, }, }] with open("train.jsonl", "w") as f: for r in csv.DictReader( open("metxbiodb.csv") ): # columns: parent_smiles, enzyme, reaction_type, metabolite_smiles user = ( f"Parent compound SMILES: {r['parent_smiles']}
Metabolising" f" enzyme: {r['enzyme']}

Reason through this biotransformation" " step by step, then call submit_metabolite with the predicted" " SMILES of the primary metabolite." ) f.write( json.dumps({ "responses_create_params": { "input": [ {"role": "system", "content": SYSTEM}, {"role": "user", "content": user}, ], "tools": TOOLS, }, "agent_ref": {"name": "metxbiodb_simple_agent"}, "parent_smiles": r["parent_smiles"], "enzyme": r["enzyme"], "reaction_type": r["reaction_type"], "ground_truth_smiles": r["metabolite_smiles"], }) + "
" ) 2. Resources server: resources_servers/metxbiodb/app.py This is the only domain-specific code, and it has three parts: Seed_session stores the ground truth per session. The submit_metabolite tool endpoint scores the first submission with Morgan-fingerprint Tanimoto (radius 2, 2048 bits). Verify returns that score as the reward. It also needs a pyproject.toml with rdkit . To adapt it to a new domain, replace tanimoto_similarity and the tool schema. 3. Registration yaml metxbiodb: resources_servers: metxbiodb: entrypoint: app.py domain: knowledge metxbiodb_simple_agent: responses_api_agents: simple_agent: entrypoint: app.py max_steps: 2 # submit, then one closing turn; only the first submission is scored resources_server: {type: resources_servers, name: metxbiodb} model_server: {type: responses_api_models, name: policy_model} datasets: - {name: train, type: example, jsonl_fpath: data/train.jsonl} - {name: validation, type: example, jsonl_fpath: data/validation.jsonl} The agent name must equal agent_ref.name in the JSONL rows. 4. Wiring in the trainer config data: train: {dataset_name: NemoGymDataset, data_path: data/train.jsonl} validation: {data_path: data/validation.jsonl} default: {dataset_name: NemoGymDataset, env_name: nemo_gym, processor: nemo_gym_data_processor, prompt_file: null, system_prompt_file: null} nemo_gym: metxbiodb_simple_agent: datasets: train: {jsonl_fpath: data/train.jsonl} validation: {jsonl_fpath: data/validation.jsonl} env: should_use_nemo_gym: true nemo_gym: config_paths: - responses_api_models/vllm_model/configs/vllm_model_for_training.yaml - resources_servers/metxbiodb/configs/metxbiodb.yaml GRPO training launch Use the following command to launch GRPO training: # Multi-node GRPO run (16 GPUs) python examples/nemo_gym/run_grpo_nemo_gym.py \ --config configs/grpo_metxbiodb_super_vl.yaml \ cluster.num_nodes=4 cluster.gpus_per_node=4 \ grpo.max_num_steps=100 Note: grpo_metxbiodb_super_vl.yaml is the complete GRPO trainer config for the environment in this blog. GRPO policy configuration The following shows the policy portion of the GRPO trainer config. The full config includes rollout, reward server, and logging settings. policy: model_name: nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3.5-Super-VL-120B-A128-BF16 tokenizer: - name: $(policy.model_name) chat_template_kwargs: null hf_config_overrides: num_next_predict_layers: 0 train_micro_batch_size: 1 logprob_batch_size: 1 logprob_chunk_size: 2048 max_total_sequence_length: 5120 make_sequence_length_divisible_by: 256 precision: bfloat16 offload_optimizer_for_logprob: true optimizer: - name: transformer_engine.pytorch.optimizers.fused_adam.FusedAdam kwargs: lr: 1.0e-06 weight_decay: 0.1 betas: [0.9, 0.999] eps: 1.0e-08 master_weights: true store_param_remainders: true exp_avg_dtype: torch.bfloat16 exp_avg_sq_dtype: torch.bfloat16 scheduler: - name: torch.optim.lr_scheduler.ConstantLR kwargs: factor: 1.0 total_iters: 1000000000 - milestones: [] automodel_cfg: enabled: true tensor_parallel_size: 1 context_parallel_size: 1 expert_parallel_size: 4 sequence_parallel: false activation_checkpointing: true cpu_offload: false moe_parallelizer: ignore_router_for_ac: true reshard_after_forward: true automodel_kwargs: backend: _target_: nemo_automodel.components.models.common.utils.BackendConfig attn: te linear: te rms_norm: torch_fp32 experts: gmm dispatcher: hybridep fake_balanced_gate: false rope_fusion: false enable_hf_state_dict_adapter: true Note: Set loss_fn.use_importance_sampling_correction=True to reduce variance between the vLLM rollout engine and the Megatron training engine. This is particularly important for mixture-of-experts (MoE) models. Distillation pipeline The following snippets score teacher rollouts by Tanimoto similarity and filter traces for the SFT corpus based on the user set threshold for the scores. The generate script and launch command look like the below: export POLICY_API_KEY=<your-inference-api-key> # Step 1: Generate rollouts from Claude Sonnet 5.5 python3 tools/generate_sft.py \ --model anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5 \ --split train \ --num-repeats 8 \ --min-reward 0.7 \ --out-dir runs/metxbiodb_sft_distilled # Step 2: Build OpenAI-format SFT dataset python3 tools/build_metxbiodb_sft_messages.py \ --in-file runs/metxbiodb_sft_distilled/generated.jsonl \ --out-dir metxbiodb/distilled_datasets \ --val-frac 0.05 \ --seed 42 Where generate_sft.py is: async def generate(row, semaphore): async with semaphore: resp = await client.responses.create( model=model, instructions=SYSTEM_PROMPT, input=[{"role": "user", "content": row["prompt"]}], tools=TOOLS, temperature=1.0, ) predicted_smiles = None response_text = getattr(resp, "output_text", "") or "" for item in getattr(resp, "output", []): if ( getattr(item, "type", None) == "function_call" and getattr(item, "name", None) == "submit_metabolite" ): predicted_smiles = ( json.loads(item.arguments).get("smiles", "").strip() ) break valid, reward = ( tanimoto(predicted_smiles, ground_truth) if predicted_smiles else (False, 0.0) ) return { "id": row["id"], "prompt": row["prompt"], "response": response_text, "predicted_smiles": predicted_smiles, "reward": reward, } Note : Filtering at score == 1.0 (exact Tanimoto match) keeps only traces where the teacher’s reasoning was chemically logical and resulted in the exact growth truth as the final answer. For softer ground truth to introduce some variations, set a threshold (e.g., score >= 0.7) instead. SFT trainer launch Use the following command to launch SFT training: # Multi-node SFT run (16 GPUs) python examples/run_sft.py \ --config configs/sft_metxbiodb_super_vl_lora.yaml \ cluster.num_nodes=4 cluster.gpus_per_node=4 \ sft.max_num_steps=55 Note: sft_metxbiodb_super_vl_lora.yaml is the complete SFT trainer config for the environment in this blog. SFT trainer configuration The following SFT trainer config warm-starts the student model on distilled traces before the RL loop begins: policy: optimizer: kwargs: lr: 1.0e-5 weight_decay: 0.0 automodel_cfg: model_save_format: safetensors checkpoint: save_consolidated: final consolidation_timeout_minutes: 120 lora_cfg: enabled: false Post-training gives research teams a way to adapt Nemotron models to the tasks and experimental evidence that matter in your domain. This recipe turns experimental records into prompts and reference answers, connects model submissions to an automated verifier, and uses reinforcement learning to train against that feedback. The central design choice is the reward: It should measure the scientific outcome you want. Teacher-model distillation offers an optional starting point, while held-out evaluation helps determine whether either training path improves performance beyond the starting model. Together, domain-specific data, task-appropriate verification, and reproducible evaluation provide a practical foundation for developing specialized scientific agents. Start with a narrowly defined task, validate the scoring method, and expand the workflow only as the evidence supports it. Get started by exploring NeMo Gym and NeMo RL , then use the accompanying recipe as an example to build and evaluate an environment for your own scientific task. Discuss (0) Like Tags Agentic AI / Generative AI | Developer Tools & Techniques | HPC / Scientific Computing | Nemotron | Intermediate Technical | Tutorial | Computational Chemistry / Materials Science | featured | Machine Learning & Artificial Intelligence | NeMo | Reinforcement Learning | SC26 | Scientific Computing About the Authors About Gautham Dharuman Gautham Dharuman is a senior architect GenAI for Science at NVIDIA, where he focuses on frontier AI models and agentic systems to accelerate scientific discovery. With extensive experience spanning national laboratories and industry, his expertise lies at the intersection of large-scale AI, post-training, and high-performance computing. He was a Gordon Bell finalist in 2024 for scaling LLM training for a protein design application that b